#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124370 :question/id #uuid "a568fc63-e81c-5210-93f0-bcbd429a9e40" :question/math-academy-id "e-6678" :question/problem "A biologist observed that the growth of the number of fish in a lake is directly proportional to the difference between the lake’s maximum fish population and the current population. Let $P(t)$ be the fish population, measured in number of fish, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum fish population that the lake can support is $200$ fish, which differential equation could be used to model the lake’s fish population? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the fish population $P(t)$ of the lake changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum fish population the lake can support and the current fish population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(a - P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum fish population the lake can support, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 200 \\textrm{ fish},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(200 - P).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a081d98d-bac7-5472-ac1f-bb81d8502479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} =-k(70 - P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121194} {:answer/id #uuid "aab37068-8f4f-5403-92f7-cb76580142a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = kP(70 - P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121195} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c5837d-8d9b-5bdc-a3d7-21a16439da00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = kP^{2}(70 - P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121196} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d3c042-9051-5258-8e61-7f0076523ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = k(70 - P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121197} {:answer/id #uuid "1efedcd4-3e2c-5390-917a-75608a7b4a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} =-k(70 + P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121198}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d06da1af-5ad6-5a97-9279-9b6036bd2a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121193}] :question/id #uuid "19a4e47b-fcd5-50aa-804a-b275eeea23d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106691" :question/problem "A factory's management noted that the growth of the unit production is directly proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Let $P(t)$ be the production quantity, measured in units produced, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in days. If the maximum number of units that a worker can produce is $70$ $units/day$, which differential equation could be used to model the factory's production? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d14aa6-7bfa-5d51-a2d3-b51157905d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(105 - R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122997} {:answer/id #uuid "8deef86d-850d-580f-936a-7b20c647e281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(1700 - R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122998} {:answer/id #uuid "034daa26-4afc-57bc-9d72-1a947446d502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(65 - R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122999} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9240f3-5806-5595-840a-e234d5f16554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(55 - R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123000} {:answer/id #uuid "4f16b14f-06bc-5798-ad2e-b30a874d50ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dR}{dt} = k(85 - R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123001}] :answer-field/id #uuid "be720ede-1dbc-51a2-b8eb-db9c18720fad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122996}] :question/id #uuid "bdadb029-2e6a-54b7-8c42-d0b37b92a134" :question/math-academy-id "q-37376" :question/problem "A rehabilitation center initially has $20$ children. The number of children in the center $R(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the center is $85$ children, which differential equation could be used to model the population of the rehabilitation center? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54f141e1-5d1e-5129-8a5d-1ce524c3a538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-k(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305973} {:answer/id #uuid "566d884b-c307-5cb5-900e-d4e916f6e60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k{N}^{2}(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305986} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ccb96d-8146-58e1-a658-cc117cb30fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-k(150+N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306742} {:answer/id #uuid "ead33e72-291a-5706-b666-4290765ed58c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307313} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e7bf3e-f305-5b82-8c10-c924a8fa96ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ead33e72-291a-5706-b666-4290765ed58c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307313} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea18a22-4aec-53d2-b22b-ca7ff8019a1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308336}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54bac72f-651e-55b5-8b4b-fbc4e753ebc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335135" :question/problem "A wildlife manager noted that the growth of the number of deer in a protected forest is directly proportional to the difference between the forest’s maximum deer population and the current population. Let $N(t)$ be the deer population, measured in number of deer, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum deer population that the forest can support is $150$ deer, which differential equation could be used to model the forest’s deer population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the deer population $N(t)$ of the forest changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum deer population the forest can support and the current deer population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-N),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum deer population the forest can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=150\\text{ deer},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04aedd39-6cd0-5b6c-a95b-8f1528463bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305194} {:answer/id #uuid "100e6d8a-679a-59a7-9f49-904c4ba960dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(30-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305298} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8045ca-7194-5335-8e8c-fb093e38eb2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(60-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305744} {:answer/id #uuid "7c223239-ad05-5364-8640-ceef9053e8b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(100-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306336} {:answer/id #uuid "eb832421-670f-5c24-a2cd-1bebc2d73fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(950-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04aedd39-6cd0-5b6c-a95b-8f1528463bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305194} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e66b97-0745-5e34-a590-bf57a3e5e855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307862}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5651275c-fd64-5799-85e0-52ab15ebd288" :question/math-academy-id "q-335142" :question/problem "A tutoring center currently has $10$ enrolled students. The number of students in the center $T(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the tutoring center is $95$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the student population of the center? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of students in the center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of students changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of students in the center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the tutoring center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=95$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(95-T).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be596d2-348f-52d1-bdbb-a8f9262ef683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(35-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305252} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc9a611-32b3-5c07-adf4-0f5db91760e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(60-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305416} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbeaf46-358d-53c4-a292-7cfd16a6e157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(150-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305460} {:answer/id #uuid "2b90d980-e2c2-5e82-8176-18a5784c6485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(250-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305569} {:answer/id #uuid "6a37b76e-f780-59e2-9d76-b4af35790d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(50-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc9a611-32b3-5c07-adf4-0f5db91760e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dM}{dt}=k(60-M)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305416} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7bbd6e9-b260-5c66-af8a-149b7483e375" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308101}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e2af3a7-6f0f-5f5a-a5bb-60da7750b37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335145" :question/problem "A marine rehabilitation aquarium initially houses $25$ marine animals. The number of animals in the aquarium $M(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $60$ animals, which differential equation could be used to model the animal population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of animals in the aquarium obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of animals changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of animals in the aquarium. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-M),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=60$ animals, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(60-M).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d8e18d-55da-5747-a381-b32f0cd64912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=-k(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315254} {:answer/id #uuid "2663e8c9-ddca-5ed4-a0c7-4cf541120ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k{F}^{2}(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315335} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1c60c4-ae35-5c6d-b92a-7fc0de21cd3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=kF(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315644} {:answer/id #uuid "653fb905-e4dc-5614-96c4-2effafd1ebcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315681} {:answer/id #uuid "a062595a-6ef4-5175-b64d-a91182de7ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=-k(100+F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "653fb905-e4dc-5614-96c4-2effafd1ebcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315681} :answer-field/id #uuid "360d624d-1168-5448-a7ea-9e7a2bd530a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316673}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2bd5b32-0a6c-5631-90f3-4c8beb909e62" :question/math-academy-id "q-344214" :question/problem "An ecologist found that the growth of the number of frogs in a wetland is directly proportional to the difference between the wetland’s maximum frog population and the current population. Let $F(t)$ be the frog population, measured in number of frogs, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum frog population the wetland can support is $100$ frogs, which differential equation could be used to model the frog population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the frog population $F(t)$ of the wetland changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum frog population the wetland can support and the current frog population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-F),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum frog population the wetland can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=100\\text{ frogs},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0259fd14-c9a9-58b6-a404-bff6dbf7badd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315128} {:answer/id #uuid "2b505a49-68dc-5562-bdf8-5c978ed54f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS(500-S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315372} {:answer/id #uuid "71a08b4a-f491-5881-ac62-23a6895de4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k{S}^{2}(500-S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315763} {:answer/id #uuid "8331eb98-12ad-5b45-8ce6-0bb6ebdd5d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k(500-S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315845} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5b4005-e626-5fa7-abfe-28e258dd834a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k(500+S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0259fd14-c9a9-58b6-a404-bff6dbf7badd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315128} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fa6037-cb1e-50be-a0f9-38bac3fc52f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316700}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab916584-a26d-54f0-907f-592aaa033fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-344218" :question/problem "A school administrator observed that the growth of the number of students enrolled in a program is directly proportional to the difference between the program’s maximum enrollment capacity and the current number of students enrolled. Let $S(t)$ be the number of students enrolled, measured in students, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of students the program can support is $500$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the number of students enrolled in the program? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of students enrolled $S(t)$ in the program changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of students the program can support and the current number of students enrolled. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-S),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of students the program can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=500\\text{ students},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f087d09-e1e7-5ab8-982f-cbc3540e7119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315209} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbca73a-2595-5694-b56c-f975654c4d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=kP(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316199} {:answer/id #uuid "d2316d69-8166-551c-a84b-3d2353b842a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316296} {:answer/id #uuid "df79cf0d-10f8-59a7-8669-02f7ce1dd947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k{P}^{2}(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316354} {:answer/id #uuid "e28e46f1-8fff-5681-9163-603d3ff9e8da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(240+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f087d09-e1e7-5ab8-982f-cbc3540e7119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315209} :answer-field/id #uuid "64f84fa9-a2f0-5ca4-b07d-e3fc3dc62fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316786}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54e7291f-5b5b-5bf3-a3dd-9eac24a3f4e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344219" :question/problem "An engineer observed that the growth of the number of solar panels installed at a facility is directly proportional to the difference between the facility’s maximum number of solar panels and the current number installed. Let $P(t)$ be the number of solar panels, measured in panels, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support is $240$ panels, which differential equation could be used to model the number of solar panels installed? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of solar panels $P(t)$ installed changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support and the current number of solar panels. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=240\\text{ panels},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a6efe89-1d82-5203-8a6a-24c18502b095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(80-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315360} {:answer/id #uuid "56b622c6-6784-5363-8e24-c96bb51d0957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(35-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315619} {:answer/id #uuid "8251af8d-948e-5507-b3a9-8ad30939c31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(25-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315838} {:answer/id #uuid "996235ec-ab0f-5b92-84a3-56a94cd4f991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(50-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315975} {:answer/id #uuid "f38c1553-bd69-57f7-ac0c-a7f412f84db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(18-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996235ec-ab0f-5b92-84a3-56a94cd4f991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(50-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315975} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05a2153-5b63-5b4c-9cfe-238a0120df42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317118}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "999dd29f-4bf1-52fd-b7b1-1ef128e903af" :question/math-academy-id "q-344228" :question/problem "A laboratory incubator initially contains $18$ bacterial cultures. The number of cultures in the incubator $B(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in days. If the maximum capacity of the incubator is $50$ cultures, which differential equation could be used to model the number of cultures? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of cultures in the incubator obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of cultures changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of cultures in the incubator. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the incubator, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=50$ cultures, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(50-B).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11749536-a0f7-5d13-b256-6fdeb5b98399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(320-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315223} {:answer/id #uuid "151ae25d-d64b-5851-adfb-806b30416fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(800-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315244} {:answer/id #uuid "5a620149-4b03-5b37-bbf4-472c6b574ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1000-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315631} {:answer/id #uuid "66e6db5c-1814-50aa-8ce2-db2edeec9f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(500-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315692} {:answer/id #uuid "c799b39e-df7d-5d1d-bea3-881572cd85f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1500-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a620149-4b03-5b37-bbf4-472c6b574ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1000-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315631} :answer-field/id #uuid "393ad745-9bcc-5868-a810-b633bf129cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316680}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3974627f-3801-52ea-b93f-3d1a3033e2cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-344233" :question/problem "A data center initially contains $320$ computers. The number of computers in the data center $C(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the data center is $1000$ computers, which differential equation could be used to model the number of computers? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of computers in the data center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of computers changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of computers in the data center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-C),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the data center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1000$ computers, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(1000-C).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f219f76-9737-5821-a859-30aa164ad6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k(90-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372693} {:answer/id #uuid "2d435dfc-8fb9-5fc0-a344-d7022166af19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2}(90-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372694} {:answer/id #uuid "57a48c5c-6c1b-59ea-991b-8f7a3b9cac74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV(90-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372695} {:answer/id #uuid "8899c662-4eda-56dd-9309-028ca91e5cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k(90+V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372696} {:answer/id #uuid "8a58c995-7408-580c-986d-705cb9bbdb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(90-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a58c995-7408-580c-986d-705cb9bbdb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(90-V)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372697} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc91c9af-72e4-5170-a068-194980f1aace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372692}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0b55ff2-bf59-5ae2-ab99-23109ff31f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-335137" :question/problem "A shipping company’s management noted that the growth of a ship’s speed is directly proportional to the difference between the ship’s maximum cruising speed and its current speed. Let $V(t)$ be the speed of the ship, measured in kilometers per hour, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in hours. If the maximum cruising speed of the ship is $90$ $\\text{km/h}$, which differential equation could be used to model the ship’s speed? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the speed $V(t)$ of the ship changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum cruising speed and the current speed. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-V),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum cruising speed of the ship, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=90\\text{ km/h},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k(90-V).\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124371 :question/id #uuid "d603efdd-6e27-5e14-a106-f9fc4a182403" :question/math-academy-id "e-6679" :question/problem "In an aquarium, there are initially $20$ fish. After $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ Assuming inhibited growth, calculate the number of fish after $100$ weeks if the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $90$ fish." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of fish $N(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total number of fish and the current number of fish. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k\\left(a-N\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 90$ fish, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(90-N).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dN}{90-N} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dN}{90-N} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90-N| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $90 - N > 0$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $N(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90-N & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 90-N & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 90-N & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ N & =90-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial number of fish was $20.$ So, we apply the initial condition $N(0)=20,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =20 \\\\ 90-K{e}^{0} & =20 \\\\ 90-K & =20 \\\\ K & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $N(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nN(t)=90 - 70 e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ So, applying the condition $N(30)=50,$ we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(30) & =50 \\\\ 90-70{e}^{-30k} & =50 \\\\ 40 & =70{e}^{-30k} \\\\ {e}^{-30k} & =\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ -30k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nN(t)=90-70e^{(t/30)\\,\\ln\\left(4/7\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally,  the number of fish after $t=100$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(100) & =90-70{e}^{(100/30)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4/7)} \\\\  & =79.161… \\\\  & ≈79\\,\\text{fish},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905 :topic/math-academy-id 1179 :topic/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Separation of Variables"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ab2b17-4102-57ca-9ab3-3ab110b225b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272469} {:answer/id #uuid "4ffdb4a5-548d-535b-93a7-3bfc67a56a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$21$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273062} {:answer/id #uuid "6023a2e0-53e0-5e0b-aabb-dd01395c05f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$17$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273215} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf7ce8a-3d39-5462-bbe2-3ed9759ce56f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273317} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8c0f40-2268-54a8-b736-b8c947a033b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bf7ce8a-3d39-5462-bbe2-3ed9759ce56f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273317} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d9f12c-be81-5b55-b153-dbf17a4401ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274836}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "754ac309-f23b-53d4-8c3a-d4c7dd3dd38f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15370" :question/problem "A University Campus has a total of $1\\,500$ students.\nAt the beginning of the new semester at the University, flu affected $300$ students. After $10$ days, the number of students affected by the flu increases to $500.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the flu-stricken campus population to reach $800$ students?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of students affected by the flu, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the campus population affected by the flu, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total campus population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total population of the campus, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,500$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,500-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,500-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,500-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,500-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,500-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial campus population affected by the flu was $300$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=300,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =300 \\\\ 1\\,500-K{e}^{0} & =300 \\\\ 1\\,500-K & =300 \\\\ K & =1\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the campus population affected by flu was $500$ students. So, applying the condition $P(10)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-10k} & =500 \\\\ 1\\,000 & =1\\,200{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $800$ students are affected by the flu. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =800 \\\\ 1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =800 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,200{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{12} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $800$ students will be affected by the flu after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6})}=29.562…≈30\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89a1b567-4d92-50bc-9b67-c3e259e0a3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$468$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123009} {:answer/id #uuid "e5843acc-3b0b-5b91-aef2-abf1ba9f4627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$489$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123010} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3ffd56-67f0-5967-8b78-0029b0163067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$420$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123011} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5bb5bc-7e52-5d0e-a372-536b69460728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$486$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123012} {:answer/id #uuid "690e1fd9-4928-5f3a-bdee-05eccf3128d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$442$ eggs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123013}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0d7ef1-07b3-513a-a823-292131ee0d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123008}] :question/id #uuid "3ce256b3-4ef6-51c4-ab72-48603b95ab11" :question/math-academy-id "q-37389" :question/problem "A poultry farmer has egg-laying chickens. She knows that her farm can produce a maximum of $500$ eggs a day; however, it is far behind it.\nSince she started monitoring, the chickens laid $390$ eggs and $400$ eggs on days $3$ and $6$, respectively. How many eggs do the chickens lay on day $42$?\nRound your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the egg production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0160e30a-5846-5aa6-86ef-a585cedd44ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$986$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321161} {:answer/id #uuid "71929081-a7d5-5326-a8f5-4740571cce74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$905$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322037} {:answer/id #uuid "78c60709-1e3a-58f3-8b53-ecf0dc4c70cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1005$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322096} {:answer/id #uuid "cd55f1ff-b05d-5c57-bf3a-581ad4b6e03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$942$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322746} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf858e4-c93a-5010-9a90-68721ca8ff55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$850$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71929081-a7d5-5326-a8f5-4740571cce74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$905$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322037} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6bfea20-78cb-5933-b3c2-3d3bdf908386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323559}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d1a929a-56b1-5075-bd38-17c32dd77741" :question/math-academy-id "q-37400" :question/problem "The population of students $P(t)$ in a school can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in years. If the initial population of the school is $800$ students, calculate the population after $4$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the population in the school is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =0.016\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =\\int 0.016\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\,500-P| & =0.016t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $2\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2500-P & ={e}^{-0.016t-C} \\\\ 2500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ 2,500-P & =K{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ P & =2\\,500-K{e}^{-0.016t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial population of the school was $800$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=800,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800 & =2\\,500-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 800 & =2\\,500-K \\\\ K & =1\\,700\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nP(t)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the population in the school after $t=4$ years is\n\n$$\nP(4)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016\\cdot 4}≈905\\,\\text{students},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0802b9a4-f869-51aa-9ad2-f5c4ec4ede2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$19$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308807} {:answer/id #uuid "79af7521-db1a-5890-848a-9723cc01378d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309697} {:answer/id #uuid "851d5afb-e8fd-5832-aa19-7aad8ee508b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309784} {:answer/id #uuid "cc811dc3-bbd1-58f9-aae7-865a2b20e03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310387} {:answer/id #uuid "d7357cc4-db50-5841-9068-42178a678657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79af7521-db1a-5890-848a-9723cc01378d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309697} :answer-field/id #uuid "099de7c6-8163-5944-a855-904c946df6a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310821}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ba6467f-d5e7-52b0-bc6e-1edae87450cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335280" :question/problem "A can of soda is taken out of a refrigerator and left in a room that stays at a constant temperature of $20{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ The soda’s temperature $T(t)$ (in degrees Celsius) can be modeled by the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes, and $k>0$ is a constant. The initial temperature of the soda is $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and after $5$ minutes, the soda has warmed to $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the soda to reach $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the can is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T).\n$$\n\nWe separate variables and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{20-T} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{20-T} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20-T| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $20-T>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20-T & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 20-T & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 20-T & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=8,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =20-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 8 & =20-K \\\\ K & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=20-12{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the temperature of the soda was $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=12,$  we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =20-12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 8 & =12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ {e}^{-5k} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -5k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$  be the time when the soda can reaches $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =16 \\\\ 20-12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =16 \\\\ 4 & =12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the value of $k,$ we get:\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}=13.547…≈14\\text{ }\\text{minutes},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156ded23-ce88-53d3-a639-9d820eb09a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308909} {:answer/id #uuid "8a644610-216c-5c70-96be-6978f05c7dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309835} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4012d6-aa43-57f5-b933-67f5ae4bf20a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309990} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fcc147-8682-560f-8b96-51d7e1858066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310305} {:answer/id #uuid "f721e0cb-70f5-51e1-acaf-e8fb8aacbddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "156ded23-ce88-53d3-a639-9d820eb09a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308909} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f6fe49-3d76-5e25-8d26-c4ee0c65a685" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310985}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "645d4a44-d927-5a86-b205-e23eb598edfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335282" :question/problem "The volume $X(t)$ (in $\\text{L}$) of water in a container as it fills toward its maximum capacity can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial volume is $1.5\\,\\text{L},$ how long does it take for the container to reach a volume of $2\\,\\text{L}?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the container's volume is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dX}{3-X} & =0.4\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dX}{3-X} & =\\int 0.4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-X| & =0.4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $3-X>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $X(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-X & ={e}^{-0.4t-C} \\\\ 3-X & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ 3-X & =K{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ X & =3-K{e}^{-0.4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial volume was $1.5\\,\\text{L}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $X(0)=1.5,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5 & =3-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 1.5 & =3-K \\\\ K & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nX(t)=3-1.5{e}^{-0.4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the volume is $2\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X({t}_{1}) & =2 \\\\ 3-1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ 1 & =1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -0.4{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =1.013… \\\\  & ≈1\\,\\text{minute},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064fbd9c-80ed-51b0-98cb-32bd23adfd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308800} {:answer/id #uuid "131e40fb-788f-5311-aa90-2ba7a9c2a470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308884} {:answer/id #uuid "604d06b0-499e-5ae6-b383-8e4f9aff6004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309476} {:answer/id #uuid "7489e527-2be8-5430-b0a7-0c197eae2f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309652} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed2b579-2a1c-5028-941b-30fd3eb330b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ed2b579-2a1c-5028-941b-30fd3eb330b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309738} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b15e461-76ed-5fe3-95c8-709b91e6e409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311033}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "169c8d2a-baf7-5d16-8418-7c154f48a72f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335578" :question/problem "Throughout an experiment in a laboratory, a culture contains $1\\,200$ bacteria in total (limited by nutrients and space). At the beginning of the experiment, $200$ of the $1\\,200$ bacteria are resistant to a new antibiotic. After $4$ hours, the number of resistant bacteria increases to $500.$ To the nearest hour, how long does it take for the number of resistant bacteria to reach $900?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of resistant bacteria, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in hours.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the resistant bacteria population, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total bacteria population in the culture and its current resistant population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total bacteria population the culture can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,200$ bacteria, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,200-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,200-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,200-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,200-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial population of resistant bacteria was $200$ cells. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=200,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K{e}^{0} & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K & =200 \\\\ K & =1\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $4$ hours, the resistant bacteria population was $500$ cells. So, applying the condition $P(4)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-4k} & =500 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,000{e}^{-4k} \\\\ {e}^{-4k} & =\\frac{700}{1\\,000}=\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ -4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $900$ bacteria are resistant to the antibiotic. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =900 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =900 \\\\ 300 & =1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{300}{1\\,000}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $900$ bacteria will be resistant to the antibiotic after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10})}{-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{10})}=13.502…≈14\\,\\text{hours},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest hour."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402135} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe81525-8780-55b2-9e79-394a40fd9a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$50$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402136} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9dd8e3-5764-58b2-9ff2-4b88c32898f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$40$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402137} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5c6a61-12a4-5352-8fcc-e3319bbc18bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402138} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb667f2-6fca-5729-a7fd-9dfe18862602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402135} :answer-field/id #uuid "63531a29-5d3e-5f5f-97ce-ef01b7d03202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402134}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4fd0915-73cc-575a-b3cc-4a156a96dd49" :question/math-academy-id "q-335542" :question/problem "A wildlife reserve can support a total of $800$ deer.\nAt the beginning of a disease study in the reserve, a parasitic infection has affected $80$ deer. After $10$ days, the number of deer affected by the parasite increases to $300.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the infected deer population to reach $600$ animals?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of deer affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the deer population affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total deer population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total deer population in the reserve, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial deer population affected by the parasite was $80$ animals. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=80,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =80 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{0} & =80 \\\\ 800-K & =80 \\\\ K & =720\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-720{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the deer population affected by the parasite was $300$ animals. So, applying the condition $P(10)=300,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =300 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-10k} & =300 \\\\ 500 & =720{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{500}{720}=\\frac{25}{36} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $600$ deer are affected by the parasite. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =600 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =600 \\\\ 200 & =720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{200}{720}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $600$ deer will be affected by the parasite after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})}=35.128…≈35\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a62be6e9-5300-5837-bb21-616f9afabd11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$570$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402142} {:answer/id #uuid "52f73195-6ad4-5063-9185-1d0ce506d0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$560$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402143} {:answer/id #uuid "487b0c65-385a-5896-a74a-7185c8309375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402144} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb7ad78-9e83-5378-a008-fb209e8c0c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$550$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402145} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3362f7-e0ab-59c9-813b-230e3fd73524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$590$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "487b0c65-385a-5896-a74a-7185c8309375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402144} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e99660a-207e-56cc-af0c-7810e8c04e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402141}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee0a87d3-d3e9-539a-9074-68a374d85d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335590" :question/problem "A small bakery produces loaves of bread. The owner knows that the bakery can produce a maximum of $600$ loaves a day; however, current production is below this maximum. Since she started monitoring, the bakers produced $480$ loaves and $500$ loaves on days $4$ and $8,$ respectively. How many loaves do they produce on day $40?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the bread production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the bread production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible bread production and its current bread production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the bakery, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ loaves, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(600-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{600-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{600-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $4,$ the bread production was $480$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(4)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & =480 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $8,$ the bread production was $500$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(8)=500,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(8) & =500 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-8k} & =500 \\\\ K{e}^{-8k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-8k}} & =\\frac{120}{100} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} & =120 \\\\ K & =120{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} \\\\ K & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=600-144{e}^{-(t/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of loaves baked on day $t=40$ is\n\n$$\nE(40)=600-144{e}^{-(40/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}≈576.743…≈577\\,\\text{loaves},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f7ba4e-e115-562d-8f0b-e2fb3c50999d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402149} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402150} {:answer/id #uuid "3c36fd19-5696-5a1e-9dc9-b04f43bf5c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402151} {:answer/id #uuid "31324eeb-37b1-5843-9dfa-024be7517c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402152} {:answer/id #uuid "11e8a292-e74d-5f79-93dd-d159881ed802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402150} :answer-field/id #uuid "450a3a23-c54b-5ed0-90a2-deef088dea63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402148}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44cded13-6bc7-52ea-8fcc-d6fe583c3d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-335619" :question/problem "A national park is monitoring the number of people who have downloaded a new park app. The system can support a maximum of $800$ users, but the total is still below this limit. Since the park began tracking downloads, the number of users reached $620$ on day $3$ and $720$ on day $9.$ On what day will the number of users reach $760?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of users, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of app users $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of users the system can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ users, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the number of users was $620.$ So, applying the condition $P(3)=620,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =620 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-3k} & =620 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the number of users was $720.$ So, applying the condition $P(9)=720,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =720 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-9k} & =720 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{180}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} & =180 \\\\ K & =180{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} \\\\ K & =180\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ K & =180\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-270{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$  be the day when the app reaches $760$ users. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =760 \\\\ 800-270{e}^{-kd} & =760 \\\\ 40 & =270{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{40}{270} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{4}{27} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the number of users will be $760$ on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})}=14.1285…≈14,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129121 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128877 :question/id #uuid "e2e5002a-d3ae-5831-b405-41618960aee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-15609" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x) = -2\\sin\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3\\right) + 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)= A\\sin(Bx +C) + D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $A=-2.$ Therefore, the amplitude is $|A|=2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048647 :topic/math-academy-id 1661 :topic/title "Vertical Stretches of Trigonometric Functions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816a4e1d-d69b-52a5-a083-0588e4b07a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128162} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8912f9-2470-59f1-81f4-c35ff720a74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128163} {:answer/id #uuid "c24339f5-5e2b-57ab-9584-16f3d693b470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128164} {:answer/id #uuid "28837de8-80ec-5647-81ce-a458630192ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128165} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb34917-5998-59ce-92ad-24caa605c5f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128166}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e8e140f3-8266-5774-9ffa-4ff3a9882441" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128161}] :question/id #uuid "4ff6e68a-88da-5e14-9ade-fe8b94a9dec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-43277" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x) = 5\\cos x$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80a2470a-f69e-54ae-a935-d38c0ebd4cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128168} {:answer/id #uuid "85439c6b-3f28-5694-ab5c-887e29b56abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128169} {:answer/id #uuid "8fdfb56b-fca0-5ef1-a0c8-76b370700b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128170} {:answer/id #uuid "b6126d03-9b54-566e-8e16-f0eaedc60110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128171} {:answer/id #uuid "0694b357-942